.include {docs}

# map_builder

`map_builder` collects Point-LIO's output into one map and one path, and saves both to disk.

**What.** One ROS 2 node (`ros2 run map_builder map_builder`). It listens to the scans and the poses
that Point-LIO publishes. It keeps the scans in a **voxel map**: space is cut into small cubes, and
each cube holds one point. It also keeps every pose, in a list. It publishes the new cubes while you
map, and on Ctrl-C it writes `map.pcd` and `trajectory.tum`.

**Why.** Point-LIO sends the same wall again with every scan, about 10 times a second. Saved as they
come, the points would pile up and the file would be huge. Point-LIO also does not save anything by
default. `map_builder` removes the repeats and keeps the result, so a walk ends with two small files
that the rest of the thesis can use.

**When.** Every time you map. `mapping.launch.py` starts it, on the robot or on the laptop.

## Where it sits

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      lio[Point-LIO] -->|"/cloud_registered"| mb[map_builder]
      lio -->|"/aft_mapped_to_init"| mb
      cfg[("map_builder.yaml")] --> mb
      mb -->|"/map_builder/delta"| viewer["viewer, later"]
      mb -->|"/map_builder/pose"| viewer
      mb -->|"/map_builder/get_map"| viewer
      mb -->|"Ctrl-C or save"| disk[("map.pcd and trajectory.tum")]

The inputs are called `cloud` and `odom` inside the node. [go2_mapping_bringup](./go2_mapping_bringup.qd)
points them at Point-LIO's two topics. The frame of the scans is `camera_init`, Point-LIO's world
frame: the origin is where the robot stood at the start, and x points where it looked.

## What it does

| Data | What the node does |
|---|---|
| Each scan | Puts every point in a cube. A cube that is already full ignores the new point |
| Each pose | Appends it to the path. A pose that is not newer than the last one is skipped |
| New cubes | Sends them as a **delta** twice a second |
| The whole map | On request: a service answers with every point. Or on a topic, if you turn it on |
| On save | Writes the map as a PCD file and the path as a TUM file |

## Quick start

With the whole chain, which is the normal way:

```bash
tools/mapping/map.sh
```

The node alone, if Point-LIO runs elsewhere:

```bash
ros2 run map_builder map_builder --ros-args \
    --params-file src/go2_mapping_bringup/config/map_builder.yaml \
    -p output_dir:=$HOME/maps \
    -r cloud:=/cloud_registered -r odom:=/aft_mapped_to_init
```

Save in the middle of a run, without stopping:

```bash
ros2 service call /map_builder/save std_srvs/srv/Trigger
```

The answer holds the folder name. A run is written to `<output_dir>/<time of the first scan, UTC>/`,
for example `~/maps/2026-10-19T14:35:23/`.

## How it works

### The code in one picture

The code is split in three parts, so that the maths does not need ROS and a test does not need PCL.
An arrow means "uses".

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      subgraph CORE["map_builder_core: plain C++"]
        vm["voxel_map: VoxelMap, Point"]
        tr["trajectory: Trajectory, Pose"]
      end
      subgraph IO["map_builder_io: needs PCL"]
        mio["map_io: make_run_folder, save_pcd, save_tum"]
      end
      subgraph EXE["executable map_builder: needs ROS"]
        main["main: spin, then save_at_exit"]
        node["node: MapBuilderNode"]
        cm["cloud_msg: from_message, to_message"]
      end
      tool["voxel_count: offline check"]
      main --> node
      node --> cm
      node --> mio
      node --> vm
      node --> tr
      cm --> vm
      mio --> vm
      mio --> tr
      tool --> vm

`voxel_count` is a small program with no ROS. It loads a PCD file and puts it in a voxel map twice.
The second time nothing must be added: it proves that no two saved points share a cube.

### Start-up

All the parameters are read-only: a later `ros2 param set` fails with an error.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant R as ROS parameters
      participant N as MapBuilderNode
      participant T as startup timer
      participant D as DDS graph
      N->>R: declare voxel_size, three rates, output_dir, save_on_exit
      N->>N: a rate of 0 or less is an error, except full_map_rate_hz
      N->>N: draw run_id, a random number
      N->>N: 2 subscriptions, 3 publishers, 2 services, 2 timers
      N->>N: full_map_rate_hz above 0: also a publisher and a timer
      N->>N: log voxel size, rates, run_id and output_dir
      T->>N: after 2 s, check_single_instance
      N->>D: how many publishers on /map_builder/delta
      D-->>N: 2 or more means another map_builder runs
      N->>N: stop with a FATAL message

The `run_id` tells a viewer that a new map has begun. The check after 2 s is there because two
`map_builder`s on the same topics would send two different maps, and a viewer cannot tell them apart.

### A cloud message

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant P as Point-LIO
      participant N as on_cloud
      participant C as cloud_msg
      participant V as VoxelMap
      P->>N: /cloud_registered
      N->>C: from_message(message)
      C-->>N: list of Point x y z intensity
      N->>N: first scan: keep frame_id and the stamp
      N->>N: later scan with another frame_id: warn and drop it
      N->>V: insert(points)
      V-->>N: how many points were new
      N->>N: remember the stamp of this scan

A cloud that cannot be read (its data is shorter than `width * height * point_step`, or a field is
missing) is logged and skipped. The first scan fixes the map frame and the name of the save folder.
Every log line "Voxels N, added M points from cloud" comes at most every 2 s.

### The voxel map

`VoxelMap::insert` does this for every point. A point that is not a number (NaN or infinity) is
skipped.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
      p["a point x, y, z"] --> f{"finite?"}
      f -->|no| skip(["skip"])
      f -->|yes| k["key = floor of x, y, z divided by voxel_size"]
      k --> e{"key already in the map?"}
      e -->|yes| ig(["ignore the point"])
      e -->|no| add["store the point under the key"]
      add --> pend["also append it to the pending list"]

`floor`, not a plain cast, because a cast rounds towards 0 and puts -0.03 and +0.03 in the same cube.
The map is a hash map (`unordered_map`) with the key hashed from three large primes, a method called
spatial hashing.

**Example.** The numbers are made up. The voxel size is 0.05 m.

| Point (x, y, z) | x, y, z divided by 0.05 | Key | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| (1.23, -0.03, 0.51) | 24.6, -0.6, 10.2 | (24, -1, 10) | new, stored |
| (1.24, -0.04, 0.52) | 24.8, -0.8, 10.4 | (24, -1, 10) | same cube, ignored |
| (1.26, -0.03, 0.51) | 25.2, -0.6, 10.2 | (25, -1, 10) | new, stored |

The first point of a cube wins. It is not averaged with the later ones.

**Real numbers.** On the straight walk `2026-10-19_line_01` (5.4 m), voxel 0.05 m gives about 44,700
points. Voxel 0.10 m gave 14,476 points on the same walk, and 0.05 m gave 45,195 on an earlier run.

### An odometry message and the three timers

Nothing is sent from the callbacks. They only store. Three timers send what was stored.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant P as Point-LIO
      participant N as MapBuilderNode
      participant S as Trajectory
      participant V as VoxelMap
      participant W as viewer
      P->>N: /aft_mapped_to_init
      N->>S: add(pose)
      S-->>N: false, if the stamp is not newer than the last one
      N->>N: keep it as the latest pose, not yet published
      Note over N: every 0.1 s, pose timer
      N->>W: /map_builder/pose, if there is a new one
      Note over N: every 0.5 s, delta timer
      N->>V: last_voxel_points_added
      V-->>N: the pending points, and the list is emptied
      N->>W: /map_builder/delta with run_id and seq

The delta holds **only the new cubes**, one point each, so it stays small. `seq` counts 1, 2, 3 and
so on inside one run. `stamp` is the newest scan inside the delta. A delta with no new points is not
sent, so a still robot sends nothing.

### How a viewer keeps in step

A viewer can join late or miss a message. The rules are in the message files `MapDelta.msg` and
`GetMap.srv`.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant W as viewer
      participant N as map_builder
      W->>N: get_map
      N->>N: send the pending points as a delta first
      N-->>W: run_id 7, seq 12, all points
      N->>W: delta seq 11
      W->>W: seq 11 is not above 12: ignore
      N->>W: delta seq 13
      W->>W: add the points
      N->>W: delta seq 15
      W->>W: 14 is missing: call get_map again
      N->>W: delta with another run_id
      W->>W: new map: throw away mine, call get_map

`get_map` first sends the pending points as a delta. This way the snapshot matches `seq` exactly, and
the viewer never has a hole between the snapshot and the next delta.

### Save

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant U as you
      participant S as save service
      participant N as MapBuilderNode
      participant M as map_io
      participant X as main
      U->>S: call save
      S->>N: on_save
      N->>M: make_run_folder(output_dir, time of the first scan)
      M-->>N: a new folder, with _1, _2 if the name exists
      N->>M: save_pcd(folder, all points)
      N->>M: save_tum(folder, trajectory)
      N-->>U: success and the folder name
      U->>X: Ctrl-C
      X->>X: spin returns
      X->>N: save_at_exit
      N->>M: the same three calls, if save_on_exit is true

Saving twice in one run gives two folders, `<time>` and `<time>_1`. Save fails with `map is empty` if
no scan came, and with `output_dir is not set` if the node has no output folder. A failed save at
exit is logged and does not crash the node.

### Types

.mermaid
    classDiagram
      class MapBuilderNode
      class VoxelMap
      class Trajectory
      class Point
      class VoxelKey
      class Pose
      MapBuilderNode *-- VoxelMap : map_
      MapBuilderNode *-- Trajectory : trajectory_
      VoxelMap *-- VoxelKey : many, one per cube
      VoxelMap *-- Point : one per key
      Trajectory *-- Pose : poses_

| File | Does |
|---|---|
| `main` | Starts the node, spins, then calls `save_at_exit` |
| `node` | The two callbacks, the timers, the services, the single-instance check |
| `cloud_msg` | `PointCloud2` message to a list of `Point` and back |
| `voxel_map` | `Point`, `VoxelKey`, the hash, and `VoxelMap` with `insert`, `last_voxel_points_added`, `all` |
| `trajectory` | `Pose`, and `Trajectory` with `add` and `write_tum` |
| `map_io` | The run folder name, the PCD writer, the TUM writer |
| `voxel_count` | Offline check of a PCD file, not part of the node |

The message types are in the small package `go2_mapping_msgs`: `MapDelta.msg` (header, `run_id`, `seq`,
a `PointCloud2` of the new points) and `GetMap.srv` (no request, answers with `run_id`, `seq` and a
`PointCloud2` of all points).

## Output files

`map.pcd` has one point per cube, binary, fields `x y z intensity`, in metres, frame `camera_init`.

`trajectory.tum` has one line per pose: `stamp x y z qx qy qz qw`. The stamp is in seconds with 9
digits after the dot. Example line, made up:

```
1760884523.123456789 1.204300 -0.031200 0.002100 0.000100 -0.002300 0.014000 0.999900
```

The path starts at (0, 0, 0). For a walk that ends on its start mark, the distance of the last pose
from the origin is the drift of Point-LIO.

## Parameters

All are read-only, read once at start-up.

| Parameter | Default | In `map_builder.yaml` | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `voxel_size` | 0.05 | 0.05 | m. Side of a cube. Must be above 0 |
| `delta_rate_hz` | 2.0 | 2.0 | How often the new cubes are sent. Above 0 |
| `pose_rate_hz` | 10.0 | 10.0 | How often the pose is sent. Above 0 |
| `full_map_rate_hz` | 0.0 | 0.0 | The whole map on a topic. 0 is off |
| `output_dir` | empty | set by the launch file | Folder for the runs. Empty makes save fail |
| `save_on_exit` | false | true | Save when you press Ctrl-C |

## Topics and services

| Direction | Name | Type | QoS |
|---|---|---|---|
| in | `cloud` | `sensor_msgs/PointCloud2` | reliable, queue 100 |
| in | `odom` | `nav_msgs/Odometry` | reliable, queue 100 |
| out | `/map_builder/delta` | `go2_mapping_msgs/MapDelta` | reliable, queue 25 |
| out | `/map_builder/pose` | `geometry_msgs/PoseStamped` | reliable, queue 1 |
| out | `/map_builder/full_map` | `sensor_msgs/PointCloud2` | reliable, transient local, queue 1. Only if the rate is above 0 |
| service | `/map_builder/get_map` | `go2_mapping_msgs/GetMap` | |
| service | `/map_builder/save` | `std_srvs/Trigger` | |

Transient local means a viewer that connects late still gets the last map. Turn `full_map_rate_hz` on
only to look at the map in RViz: every message holds the whole map.

## Build and test

```bash
cd ~/unitree_go2
source ~/unitree_ros2/setup.sh
vcs import src < go2.repos
colcon build --packages-up-to map_builder
```

It needs PCL (the point cloud library) for the PCD file. The package has no unit tests yet. It was
checked by replaying a bag through the whole chain on the laptop. The steps are in
`src/map_builder/README.md`. A correct run of `2026-10-19_line_01` at `-r 0.25` gives:

| Check | Real result |
|---|---|
| Points in `map.pcd` | 44,641 and 44,729 in two runs |
| Lines in `trajectory.tum` | 297 |
| x of the last pose | 5.33 to 5.35 m, the tape says 5.40 m |
| `ros2 run map_builder voxel_count <map.pcd> 0.05` | `second inserted: 0` |
| Last `seq` | about 150, depends on timing |

If the map has about 18,700 points and the end is at x 7.36 m, Point-LIO ran with the old 0.5 m filter
size. Check `filter_size_surf` and `filter_size_map` in `point_lio.yaml`.

## Common problems

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `another map builder is running`, then the node stops | An old `map_builder` is still alive | `pgrep -af map_builder`, stop it, start again |
| `save` answers `map is empty` | No scan arrived | Check `ros2 topic hz /cloud_registered` |
| `Frame ID mismatch: expected camera_init` | Something else publishes on `/cloud_registered` | `ros2 topic info -v /cloud_registered` |

## Known gaps

- **No unit tests.** `VoxelMap`, `Trajectory` and `map_io` are plain C++ and easy to test. The CMake
  file has the lines ready but commented out. The only checks are the bag replay and `voxel_count`.
- **The first point of a cube stays, the rest are lost.** There is no average. A noisy first point
  stays in the map for the whole run.
- **Nothing is ever removed.** A person who walks through the scene leaves points that stay in the
  map. Point-LIO has no loop closing either, so drift is not corrected later.
- **A pose can be published and not saved.** `on_odometry` ignores the answer of `Trajectory::add`.
  A pose that is not newer than the last is skipped in the file but still goes to `/map_builder/pose`.
- **A save is lost if the node is killed.** The map is only in memory. `save_on_exit` works on
  Ctrl-C. A crash or `kill -9` loses the run, unless `save` was called before.
- **A scan with another frame is dropped with a warning only.** The warning comes at most every 2 s.
- **Two nodes starting together.** The check counts publishers after 2 s. From reading the code, not
  from a test, two nodes that start together could both see two and both stop.
